{% extends "doc_page.jinja" %}
{% import "chrome.jinja" as chrome %}
{% import "components.jinja" as ui %}
{% import "forthcoming_data.jinja" as fc %}
{% block page_title %}States and Probes (Forthcoming) - Netsuke Build System{% endblock %}
{% block content %}

    <div class="site-container pt-16 grow flex">

        {{ fc.sidebar('states') }}

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <main id="main-content" class="hm-docs-content flex-1 min-w-0 py-12 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-12">

            {% call fc.header('states') %}
                    A directory timestamp cannot prove that a virtual environment holds the right interpreter, and last week's successful
                    install cannot prove that a service still answers. RFC 0021 proposes optional named states: a condition, the evidence
                    that checks it, and an optional recipe that prepares it &mdash; verified every time, prepared at most once, and never
                    repaired behind anyone's back.
            {% endcall %}

            <section id="status" class="mb-16">
                <div class="bg-warning/5 border border-warning/30 rounded-xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="font-bold text-base-content text-sm uppercase tracking-wide mb-3 flex items-center">
                        <span class="iconify text-warning mr-2" data-icon="carbon:information-filled" aria-hidden="true"></span> Status on {{ generated_at.day }} {{ generated_at.strftime('%B %Y') }}
                    </h2>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-accent mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:close" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Not in v{{ netsuke_version }}.</strong> Nothing on this page is implemented. The released compiler does not accept <code>states</code>, <code>ensure_state</code>, or any other syntax shown here.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:document" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Proposed.</strong> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0021-managed-states-and-probes.md" class="hm-link">RFC 0021</a> has status Proposed, created 19 September 2026. It landed in <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/commit/54837e9" class="hm-link">commit 54837e9</a>, which its own message calls documentation only: no runtime implementation, schema changes, or accepted ADRs.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-warning mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:task" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>Planned as phase 23.</strong> The <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#23-verified-preparation-without-mandatory-environments" class="hm-link">progressive-enhancement roadmap</a> splits the work into eight tasks; none is ticked. The roadmap says it &ldquo;tracks proposed work, not implemented features&rdquo;.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:tag" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><strong>No release named.</strong> The RFC calls its examples &ldquo;proposed fragments, not current-release promises&rdquo;, and leaves the <code>python-venv</code> inspection contract, the portable lease implementation, record retention limits, and operator probe-policy fields to be ratified before implementation.</span></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="why" class="mb-16">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:idea" aria-hidden="true"></span> Why states
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Today a manifest expresses preparation as an ordinary command &mdash; <code>command: uv sync</code> &mdash; and Ninja decides
                    freshness by timestamp. That is fine until the question stops being &ldquo;is this file newer?&rdquo; and becomes &ldquo;is
                    this environment usable?&rdquo;. States are, informally, the sensors of the system; Netsuke's own vocabulary is
                    <em>states</em>, <em>probes</em>, and <em>external functional checks</em>, and the rest of this page uses it.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Timestamps are not evidence</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">&ldquo;A directory timestamp cannot establish that a virtual environment contains the requested interpreter and packages.&rdquo; A state checks the thing itself, on every use.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Guards must not build</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">&ldquo;A test that requires an already-installed extension must not silently build it.&rdquo; A requirement that quietly repairs itself tests the repair, not the precondition.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Not a new scheduler</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">State operations are execution units on RFC 0001's <a href="{{ fc.href('structured-commands') }}" class="hm-link">structured runner</a>, not a template-time subprocess facility. Cargo, uv, and friends keep dependency resolution and incremental compilation.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="declaring" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:dashboard" aria-hidden="true"></span> Declaring a state
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    A state definition carries <code>kind</code>, kind-specific configuration, an optional <code>path</code>, <code>identity</code>,
                    and optional <code>prepare</code>, <code>probe</code>, and <code>accept_degraded</code>. <code>kind</code> selects a documented
                    built-in probe; there is no <code>probe: builtin</code> boilerplate. The smallest useful state checks that a directory exists:
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Proposed syntax, RFC 0021 &sect;3 (verbatim)</p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-4" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
states:
  build-directory:
    kind: directory
    path: build
    prepare:
      invoke: python -c "from pathlib import Path; Path('build').mkdir(exist_ok=True)"

actions:
  - name: prepare-directory
    command:
      ensure_state: build-directory
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-8 max-w-3xl">It promises only that <code>build</code> is a directory, &ldquo;not that its contents form a valid build&rdquo;. Unknown fields, duplicate names, unresolved references, and unsupported kinds fail before execution.</p>

                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Kind</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">What the built-in probe claims</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows">
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">directory, file</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">The declared object has that type, checked without following symlinks. No content-freshness claim.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">python-venv</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">The actual interpreter, its environment prefix, and any declared interpreter constraint. It &ldquo;must not advertise package-set verification that it does not implement.&rdquo;</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">custom</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Nothing of its own. Requires an explicit external probe and cannot claim more than that probe supplies. May omit <code>path</code> to observe a service, which does not authorize network access or remote mutation.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="probes" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:activity" aria-hidden="true"></span> Probes and external functional checks
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Built-in probes serve the ordinary case. When a project needs a condition no built-in knows about, it adds an external
                    functional check: a repository-owned executable that reports through the optional <code>nagios</code> protocol. No Nagios
                    installation, daemon, or metric parser is involved &mdash; only the plugin exit-status convention.
                </p>
                <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Proposed syntax, RFC 0021 &sect;3 (verbatim)</p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-4" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
states:
  dev-env:
    kind: python-venv
    path: .venv
    identity:
      files: [pyproject.toml, uv.lock, tools/check_dev_env.py]
    prepare:
      invoke: uv sync --locked --group dev
    probe:
      external:
        invoke: python tools/check_dev_env.py
      protocol: nagios

actions:
  - name: test
    command:
      - ensure_state: dev-env
      - invoke: uv run --no-sync pytest
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-8 max-w-3xl">
                    The built-in environment checks still run. The external check adds a condition; &ldquo;it cannot bypass the built-in checks
                    or forge preparation identity.&rdquo; The script is project code, not shipped tooling, and a lockfile digest records what
                    preparation was given, not whether the installation is still intact.
                </p>

                <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Exit codes</h3>
                <div class="bg-white rounded-lg border border-base-300 shadow-xs overflow-x-auto [contain:inline-size] mb-6" tabindex="0">
                    <table class="min-w-full hm-rows prop-table">
                        <caption class="visually-hidden">Nagios-style statuses and their Netsuke interpretation, RFC 0021 table 1</caption>
                        <thead class="bg-base-100">
                            <tr>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Exit</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Probe result</th>
                                <th scope="col" class="px-6 py-3 text-left text-xs font-bold text-base-content uppercase tracking-wider">Default state-operation behaviour</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white hm-rows">
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono text-charcoal-mid">0</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-success-text">ready</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Continue only if every other condition passes.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono text-charcoal-mid">1</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-warning-text">degraded</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Stop without repair; explicit acceptance may continue.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono text-charcoal-mid">2</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-primary">not_ready</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">An ensure may prepare once; a requirement never prepares.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono text-charcoal-mid">3</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 whitespace-nowrap text-sm font-mono font-medium text-base-content">unknown</td>
                                <td class="px-6 py-4 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">Stop without repair.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mb-6">Table from RFC 0021 &sect;6. The statuses follow the <a href="https://nagios-plugins.org/doc/guidelines.html" class="hm-link">Nagios plugin guidelines</a>.</p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">The exit status is authoritative</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Any other exit, a signal, a spawn failure, a timeout, malformed output encoding, or an exhausted output budget is <code>unknown</code>, with a distinct reason. &ldquo;A success-looking message cannot convert a failing exit into readiness.&rdquo; The first stdout line is a human summary; a <code>|</code> performance suffix may be kept but never controls anything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Bounded, and honest about it</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Proposed defaults: a ten-second deadline and 64&nbsp;KiB of combined output, adjustable with <code>probe.timeout_seconds</code> and <code>probe.max_output_bytes</code> under operator caps. Only <code>invoke</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>cwd</code>, and an allowed <code>shell</code> are accepted in <code>probe.external</code>. The runner &ldquo;must not claim a read-only or network sandbox that it does not supply.&rdquo;</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="operations" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> Require, ensure, prepare
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    The proposal adds exactly three single-key operations to the structured command union. Readiness is defined separately from
                    repair, and there is &ldquo;no automatic repair loop or fallback runner.&rdquo;
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <div class="font-mono text-sm text-primary mb-2">require_state</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Verifies readiness and never prepares anything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <div class="font-mono text-sm text-primary mb-2">ensure_state</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Verifies; prepares once when the state is definitely not ready and preparation is declared and authorized; then verifies again.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <div class="font-mono text-sm text-primary mb-2">prepare_state</div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Explicitly runs preparation and then verification, leaving incremental work to the ecosystem tool. A separate authorized request, not a consequence of a failed probe.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Adapted from RFC 0021 &sect;5 and &sect;9; the <code>built-extension</code> definition is omitted</p>
                <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size] mb-4" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
actions:
  - name: extension-tests
    command:
      - require_state: built-extension
      - invoke: uv run --no-sync pytest tests/extension
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    This is the guard the RFC's Cuprum canary wants: <code>require_state</code> reports a missing extension without running
                    Maturin to build it. Failure stops the rest of the action, and a state with no preparation recipe yields &ldquo;an actionable
                    unmet-precondition result, not an inferred installer.&rdquo;
                </p>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:information" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>The result algebra.</strong> <code>ready</code>, <code>not_ready</code>, <code>degraded</code>, and <code>unknown</code>.
                        <code>degraded</code> fails a requirement by default and never triggers repair; <code>accept_degraded: true</code> may admit it
                        within operator policy, and the warning stays visible. <code>unknown</code> never authorizes preparation, and an inspection error
                        outranks a repair decision: one unknown check blocks repair even when another says not-ready.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="evidence" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:locked" aria-hidden="true"></span> Evidence and leases
                </h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Identity</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-4">
                            <code>identity.files</code> lists exact paths, not shell patterns. Identity covers the normalized definition, the probe
                            implementation and version, the preparation plan, relevant declared input values, the declared files' contents, and the
                            resolved execution settings; it excludes irrelevant <a href="{{ fc.href('typed-inputs', 'callers') }}" class="hm-link">typed inputs</a>.
                            Files an external probe uses must participate. Diagnostics distinguish a declared command from a verified executable and
                            disclose unmodelled ambient dependencies.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">Records</h3>
                        <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid mb-4">
                            A persistent record &ldquo;proves only that a particular preparation completed and passed verification. It is never a
                            reusable live probe result.&rdquo; Mutable state is validated on every operation: no timestamp stamps, no unqualified success
                            stamps, no default time-to-live. A removed or corrupted record means no trusted evidence, not success. Records are published
                            atomically, only after verification, so interrupted preparation leaves none.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Leases</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Before its first command, an action acquires a workspace-scoped advisory lease for every state it references, in canonical order, and holds them through its last consumer. They are integrity locks for cooperating invocations; unmanaged commands and other programs do not take part, and the guarantee must say so.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Scheduling</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">State operations stay inside ordinary Ninja-scheduled action edges. State evidence is not a Ninja output, so a state-using action must be always-run; an incremental file target with state operations is rejected rather than risk skipping a readiness check because an unrelated output is up to date.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Cleanup and teardown</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">No automatic removal after failure and no undeclared teardown. <a href="{{ fc.href('artefacts', 'lifecycle') }}" class="hm-link">Artefact ownership</a> is a separate optional contract; when an owned environment is cleaned, its records are invalidated under the same lease before anything is deleted.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Roadmap: <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#232-execute-leased-state-operations-with-durable-evidence" class="hm-link">23.2, leased state operations with durable evidence</a>, building on the shared lease seam in <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#202-make-the-common-boundaries-independently-consumable" class="hm-link">20.2</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="shallow-end" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:flow" aria-hidden="true"></span> From the shallow end
                </h2>
                <div class="bg-primary/5 border border-primary/20 rounded-lg p-5 flex items-start mb-8">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-3" data-icon="carbon:security" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                        <strong>The guarantee belongs to <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#2-the-shallow-end-contract" class="hm-link">RFC 0025</a>.</strong>
                        Unused state declarations never cause runtime probes, installation, network access, or resource acquisition. No heuristic
                        promotes <code>uv sync</code> to a state. The quickstart manifest stays a release-acceptance fixture, and basic usage is
                        &ldquo;a supported destination, not a temporary migration stage.&rdquo; RFC 0021 defers to it by name.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-8">
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">What stays optional</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>A plain <code>command: uv sync</code> remains valid. A state earns its place only when an author needs an explicit precondition, validated reuse, or a shared preparation contract.</span></li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Adoption is per action. No context, typed input, ownership declaration, maturity policy, or bundle is compulsory.</span></li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-success mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Help, <code>check</code>, graph generation, context inspection, and dry-run never run a probe or a preparation.</span></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="min-w-0">
                        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-charcoal-mid mb-2">Quickstart manifest, as RFC 0025 &sect;2 fixes it</p>
                        <div class="bg-base-content rounded-lg p-4 overflow-x-auto min-w-0 [contain:inline-size]" tabindex="0">
{% highlight 'netsuke' %}{% raw %}
netsuke_version: "1.0.0"

targets:
  - name: hello.txt
    command: "echo 'Hello from Netsuke!' > hello.txt"

defaults:
  - hello.txt
{% endraw %}{% endhighlight %}
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-2">A normal hello-world build must start zero probes and create no state records.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-4 text-sm uppercase tracking-wide">The route in, one step at a time</h3>
                <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm text-charcoal-mid list-decimal pl-5 max-w-3xl">
                    <li>A <code>directory</code> or <code>file</code> state with <code>ensure_state</code>: presence, nothing more.</li>
                    <li>A <code>python-venv</code> state with <code>identity</code> and <code>prepare</code>, so preparation is recorded against its inputs.</li>
                    <li><code>require_state</code> on guards that must never build what they check.</li>
                    <li>An external functional check under the <code>nagios</code> protocol, then <code>kind: custom</code> for conditions no built-in covers.</li>
                    <li>The opt-in <code>verified-states</code> <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#5-initial-rule-contracts" class="hm-link">maturity rule</a>, for a project that wants selected actions checked for a state operation before their first command.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid mt-4">The RFC's delivery order matches: &ldquo;built-in probes and explicit operation semantics first, then external functional checks&rdquo;. See roadmap <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#231-define-readiness-separately-from-execution-and-repair" class="hm-link">23.1</a> and <a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#233-admit-external-functional-checks-without-implicit-repair" class="hm-link">23.3</a>.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="confidence" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:certificate-check" aria-hidden="true"></span> Confidence
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    Every item below is a planned acceptance requirement from RFC 0021 &sect;9 and roadmap phase 23. None exists yet. The roadmap asks
                    for Proptest over algebraic invariants, bounded Kani harnesses for pure transition logic where useful, and subprocess end-to-end
                    tests for process, filesystem, and locking boundaries.
                </p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Properties</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Unknown never authorizes repair; degraded requires explicit acceptance; ensure performs at most one preparation attempt; only verified success creates a record.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">Hostile probes</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Every exit code, empty and multiline output, performance suffixes, non-UTF-8 data, terminal escapes, huge simultaneous output, hung descendants, cancellation, and &ldquo;a failure message containing apparent success text&rdquo;. Collection must stay bounded and every process reaped.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">End to end</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">Delete a package or interpreter component after successful preparation; change identity inputs; contend on one resource from separate processes; interrupt between preparation and record publication.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-base-300 shadow-xs min-w-0">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-base-content mb-2 text-sm">A real migration</h3>
                        <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid">The Cuprum canary (<a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/roadmap-progressive-enhancement.md#234-validate-state-value-in-a-real-migration" class="hm-link">roadmap 23.4</a>) must show fresh detection of a removed component, never a stale probe counted as a cache hit, and zero probes for the unchanged hello-world selection.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p class="text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">The limits are stated as plainly as the guarantees. &ldquo;State annotations do not make an untrusted checkout safe to execute.&rdquo; An observational convention &ldquo;must not be described as an operating-system sandbox.&rdquo;</p>
            </section>

            <section id="access" class="mb-16 pt-10 border-t border-stone-light">
                <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-base-content mb-6 flex items-center">
                    <span class="iconify text-primary mr-3 text-lg" data-icon="carbon:accessibility" aria-hidden="true"></span> Accessibility and localization
                </h2>
                <p class="text-charcoal-mid mb-6 max-w-3xl">
                    RFC 0021 does not mention accessibility. It makes no promise about how state results appear in Netsuke's
                    <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#accessible-output" class="hm-link">accessible output mode</a>, and this page does not invent one.
                    What the design does say bears on anyone reading probe output through a screen reader or a log:
                </p>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl mb-6">
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Probe arguments and output are redacted through the shared diagnostic policy, messages are bounded, and terminal control sequences are escaped. Raw probe output never becomes a metric label.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>The first stdout line of an external probe is a human summary; the rest is bounded diagnostic data. A preparation-identity mismatch and a functional failure keep distinct reason codes.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span>Roadmap task 23.4.2 publishes state diagnostics and a probe author guide, and adds &ldquo;metadata and localization through existing surfaces&rdquo; &mdash; the <a href="/netsuke/docs/cli/#localization" class="hm-link">Fluent catalogues</a> Netsuke already ships.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start"><span class="iconify text-primary mt-1 mr-2 text-xs" data-icon="carbon:checkmark" aria-hidden="true"></span> <span><a href="https://github.com/leynos/netsuke/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0025-progressive-enhancement-and-maturity-policies.md#8-evaluation-and-diagnostics" class="hm-link">RFC 0025 &sect;8</a> tells maturity diagnostics, including <code>verified-states</code> findings, to &ldquo;Reuse Fluent localization&rdquo; and never to leak probe output.</span></li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-xs text-charcoal-mid max-w-3xl">Probe authors write their own summary line; the RFC says nothing about localizing it.</p>
            </section>

{{ fc.footer('states') }}

        </main>
    </div>

